<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romania is a country in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, formed in the nineteenth century from the union of the principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was greatly enlarged with territories, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, taken from the dissolved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>. Its lands were earlier part of the cultural and religious sphere of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, as accounts of the Byzantine Empire note.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romania.jpg" alt="Romania" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AdiJapan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Romania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Romania lies on the lower course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and on the northwestern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. It covers 238,397 square kilometres, and its terrain is distributed roughly equally between mountains, hills and plains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a> dominate the centre, with 14 ranges reaching above 2,000 metres; the highest is Moldoveanu Peak at 2,544 metres. They are surrounded by the Moldavian and Transylvanian plateaus, the Pannonian Plain and the Wallachian plains.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and forms the Danube Delta, the second-largest and best-preserved delta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. At 5,800 square kilometres it is the largest continuous marshland in Europe. Romania has one of the largest areas of undisturbed forest in Europe, covering almost 27% of its territory, and about 50% of Europe's brown bears outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human remains from Peștera cu Oase, about 40,000 years old, are the oldest known Homo sapiens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Salt production at the Poiana Slatinei site began between the 5th and 4th millennium BCE, the earliest evidence for salt exploitation in Europe, and the Cucuteni-Trypillia culture flourished in the 3rd millennium BCE. Greek colonies were established on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast in the 7th century BCE. Burebista, the first Dacian ruler to unite the local tribes, conquered the Greek colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> between around 55 and 44 BCE, and his kingdom collapsed after he was murdered in 44 BCE.</p><p>Part of the territory became the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-dacian-wars">Trajan's Dacian Wars</a>. The modern state was formed in 1859 through the union of Moldavia and Wallachia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandru-ioan-cuza">Alexandru Ioan Cuza</a> and took the name Romania in 1866. The Great Union of 1918 and the treaties that followed created Greater Romania, which reached its largest territorial extent. King Michael I was forced to abdicate in December 1947, and the communist Romanian People's Republic followed. The revolution of 1989 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Romania is a semi-presidential republic with a bicameral Parliament of a Senate and a Chamber of Deputies. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2007 and is a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 19,053,815 people, of whom 89.33% were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, 6.05% Hungarians and 3.44% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> puts the Roma figure higher, at 8.32%.</p><p>The name derives from the endonym român, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> romanus. The oldest surviving document in Romanian is Neacșu's letter of 1521, which mentions Țeara Rumânească, the Romanian Land, meaning Wallachia. About 12 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and people with Romanian-born ancestors live abroad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Romania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's break-up in 1918 created or enlarged seven states, Austria, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, Poland, <b>Romania</b> and Italy, along lines that satisfied the principle of national self-determination only imperfectly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orthodox Christianity that the Byzantine state patronised and defined became the faith of Russia, Serbia, Bulgaria, <b>Romania</b>, Georgia and the Greek world, a “Byzantine commonwealth” that extended the empire's cultural reach far beyond its political frontiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Sigmaringen branch, created in the division of 1576, produced Charles Anthony, minister-president of Prussia from 1858 to 1862, and his son Charles, who became Prince of <b>Romania</b> in 1866 and King Carol I in 1881, founding the Romanian royal house.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Republic relied on alliances: the Little Entente with Yugoslavia and <b>Romania</b>, a treaty with France in 1924 and a pact with the Soviet Union in 1935 that obliged Moscow to help only if France acted first.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Basic treaties with <b>Romania</b>, Slovakia and Ukraine renounced territorial claims, though the rights of the roughly five million Hungarians living abroad, mostly in Romania, Slovakia and Serbia, still cause tensions; relations with Serbia grew close, while those with Ukraine deteriorated from 2017 over the Hungarian minority there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Greece was the first new nation state carved out of the Ottoman Empire, and its creation by the intervention and guarantee of the great powers set a pattern followed by Serbia, <b>Romania</b> and Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
